/-! # Order generators in an arbitrary normal-cone presentation

Source: Hironaka, Certain numerical characters of singularities, Theorem IV;
Dietel (2014), Theorem 9.2.2 and embedding comparison 9.1.8, pp.108--109.
(blueprint: lem-trf-near-point-order-presentation).
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v
open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertSamuel

/-- The homogeneous prime of a near point is relevant, and in any graded presentation
its kernel is generated by positive-degree forms of local order at least their degree.
This is only the numerical presentation input; it asserts no additive symmetry.

Source: the minimal-presentation reduction and local fraction map in steps 1--3 of
lem-trf-near-point-additive-symmetry; Hironaka Theorem IV, Dietel Theorem 9.2.2 and 9.1.8.
(blueprint: lem-trf-near-point-order-presentation).

Proof: The canonical Rees quotient Rees/m Rees = k tensor_(R/I) gr_I R sends the
homogeneous prime of y to a homogeneous relevant prime. Its ideal image is the span
of precisely the homogeneous initial forms displayed below: split every element
of the homogeneous prime into components and use surjectivity of the quotient on
each degree. Pullback under the graded surjection gives q. Thus q is prime,
homogeneous, and relevant. In particular there is a variable, so N is positive.
The base-coefficient evaluation in k(y) equals the composition of residue-field
maps used in the summation exponent, by naturality of evaluation. Quotient induction
on R/m identifies this action with blowupPointResidueAlgebra, hence identifies the
two transcendence degrees.
Choose a basis V of the degree-one kernel and lifts X of a basis of the degree-one
fibre. The resulting invertible linear substitution gives P=Q[V]. Evaluation V=0
shows K=(V)+JP and q=(V)+q0 P. The presentation Q onto the fibre is minimal and
satisfies the gradedness and canonical homogeneous-representative conditions of
Hironaka.nearBlowup_order_generators. Apply that theorem with the identified
residue action and near equality. It generates J by positive-degree homogeneous
H with H/1 in (q0 Q_q0)^degree(H). The inclusion Q into P extends to a local map
Q_q0 into P_q and takes these powers into the corresponding powers of q P_q.
Each V also has degree one and belongs to q. Thus H and V generate K and satisfy
the stated order condition. They are contained in the displayed generating set,
whereas every member of that set belongs to K. The two ideal inclusions give the
equality below. Finally undo the invertible linear substitution; it preserves
degree, prime localization and its ideal powers. This includes zero kernels and
redundant variables, and does not require k(y) to equal k. -/
theorem near_point_order_presentation
    {F : Type v} [Field F] {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsReduced R] [Algebra F R]
    [Algebra.EssFiniteType F R]
    (I : Ideal R) (hI : I ≤ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) [I.IsPrime]
    [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ I)]
    (hcomponents : ∀ p ∈ minimalPrimes R, ¬ I ≤ p)
    [Module.Flat (R ⧸ I) (Ideal.associatedGraded I)]
    (y : AffineBlowup I)
    (hy : AffineBlowup.toSpec I y = IsLocalRing.closedPoint R)
    (hnear : (summation^[(letI := ((((Spec (.of R)).residueFieldCongr hy.symm).hom ≫
        (AffineBlowup.toSpec I).residueFieldMap y).hom.comp
          (IsLocalRing.ResidueField.mapEquiv
            (stalkClosedPointIso (.of R)).symm.commRingCatIsoToRingEquiv).toRingHom).toAlgebra
        Cardinal.toNat (Algebra.trdeg (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)
          ((AffineBlowup I).residueField y)))]) (localFunction (AffineBlowup I) y) =
      fun n : ℕ ↦ (Module.length R
        (R ⧸ (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) ^ (n + 1))).toNat)
    (N : ℕ)
    (φ : MvPolynomial (Fin N) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) →ₐ[IsLocalRing.ResidueField R]
      Ideal.normalConeFibre I hI)
    (hφ : Function.Surjective φ)
    (hgraded : ∀ i : Fin N, φ (MvPolynomial.X i) ∈
      Submodule.span (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)
        (Set.range (Ideal.normalConeFibreClass I hI 1))) :
    let q := Ideal.comap φ.toRingHom (Ideal.span {z | ∃ (n : ℕ) (a : (I ^ n : Ideal R)),
      reesMonomial I a.val a.property ∈ y.asHomogeneousIdeal.toIdeal ∧
        z = Ideal.normalConeFibreClass I hI n a})
    1 ≤ N ∧ ∃ hq : q.IsPrime,
      letI := hq
      (∀ f ∈ q, ∀ d : ℕ, MvPolynomial.homogeneousComponent d f ∈ q) ∧
      (¬ @HomogeneousIdeal.toIdeal _ _ _ _ _ _
        (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (Fin N) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)) _ _
        MvPolynomial.gradedAlgebra (@HomogeneousIdeal.irrelevant _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _
          (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (Fin N) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))
          MvPolynomial.gradedAlgebra) ≤ q) ∧
      RingHom.ker φ.toRingHom = Ideal.span
        {H : MvPolynomial (Fin N) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) | φ H = 0 ∧
          ∃ d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ H.IsHomogeneous d ∧
            algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin N) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))
              (Localization.AtPrime q) H ∈
                (q.map (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin N) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))
                  (Localization.AtPrime q))) ^ d} := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertSamuel
